                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Ghosts of Miami came out back in 2017 and barely has any reviews, so hopefully mine sheds a light on the game. \n\nI loved the 80s setting, the art, and the different characters. I also liked what the game was trying to do as a detective visual novel, even if the execution felt uneven. Some parts were handled really well, while others felt underdeveloped. I honestly think the romance could’ve been left out completely.\n\nThe detective side also wasn’t quite what I expected. The clues you collect don’t seem to matter nearly as much as the choices you make right at the end of each case. There were also times when the game showed me places I could supposedly visit but wouldn’t actually let me choose them, so some of the freedom feels more like the illusion of choice. The dialogue can be like that too.\n\nToward the end, things also get confusing without much guidance on how you’re supposed to figure everything out.\n\nAnd the soundtrack? Awful. I eventually just muted it.\n\nIt’s cheap for the amount of content you get, and I don’t think it’s a bad game. I just kept seeing the potential for something better than what it actually turned into.",
